//! issue_recheck — re-test one "this thing is gone" issue claim, and refuse to
//! answer at all when the search that would answer it cannot be trusted.
//!
//! A large share of our open issues are one shape: someone reported that a
//! symbol, a knob, or a relation is missing, and the only honest way to keep
//! that report alive is to go and look again. Looking again by hand is where
//! the mistake lives — a search pointed at the wrong directory returns zero
//! hits, which reads exactly like a symbol that is genuinely absent. The
//! comfortable number and the true number are the same number.
//!
//! So this asks a question it already knows the answer to first, and reports
//! nothing at all if that one comes back wrong.
//!
//! WHAT IT DOES NOT DO: this measures whether a literal string appears under a
//! path. That is evidence about a claim of absence; it is not the claim itself.
//! A feature can be present under another name, and a symbol can survive in a
//! comment. Read the finding as what it says and no further.
workflow issue_recheck
  input issue: String
  input scope: String
  input claim: String
  input control_symbol: String
  outcome standing:   type Verdict, route success
  outcome unmeasured: type Refused, route failure

/// What the search hands back. Only stdout is read: a search's EXIT STATUS is
/// not its answer, and reading it as one is precisely how a broken search comes
/// back "clean".
type Found { stdout: String }

/// A re-test that actually happened. `control_hits` travels with it so the
/// answer carries its own proof that the search could find anything at all —
/// an assertion of a working method is not the same as a demonstration of one.
type Verdict {
  issue: String,
  scope: String,
  symbol: String,
  hits: String,
  control_hits: String,
  finding: String,
}

/// A re-test that did not happen, said out loud rather than reported as a pass.
type Refused { issue: String, scope: String, reason: String }

worker recheck
  /// How many files under `scope` contain `needle`, as a literal string.
  ///
  /// Always exits 0 on purpose. `grep` exits 1 when it matches nothing, and a
  /// non-zero exit in a declared body is retryable by default — so an honest
  /// "not found" would be retried as though it were a busy disk. Ending the
  /// pipeline in `printf` swallows the status and leaves the COUNT as the one
  /// thing this action reports.
  action search(needle: String, scope: String) -> Found
    run "sh -c 'grep -rlF -- \"$1\" \"$2\" 2>/dev/null | wc -l | xargs printf %s' -- $needle $scope"

/// Ask a question whose answer is already known, before asking the one that
/// matters.
///
/// A missing directory, a mistyped path, and a tree the process cannot read all
/// return zero hits — the same answer a genuinely absent symbol returns. Nothing
/// downstream can tell those apart, so the distinction is made here or not at
/// all.
step control
  search(needle: control_symbol, scope: scope) -> anchor
  outcome broken: when anchor.stdout == "0",
    route unmeasured(issue: issue, scope: scope,
      reason: "the control symbol was not found either, so this search cannot tell an absent symbol from a failed search — no finding is reported")
  outcome measurable: otherwise, route probe

/// Now the real question, on a search that has just proved it can find things.
step probe
  search(needle: claim, scope: scope) -> hits
  outcome still_absent: when hits.stdout == "0",
    route standing(issue: issue, scope: scope, symbol: claim, hits: hits.stdout,
      control_hits: anchor.stdout,
      finding: "the symbol does not appear anywhere under this scope, on a search proved able to find things")
  outcome now_present: otherwise,
    route standing(issue: issue, scope: scope, symbol: claim, hits: hits.stdout,
      control_hits: anchor.stdout,
      finding: "the symbol DOES appear under this scope — a claim that it is missing is dead as written")
